 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Spherical Harmonics Approximation](8741-spherical-harmonics-approximation.md)
-Spherical Harmonics (SH) Approximation is a technique to represent directional signals as a linear combination of orthogonal basis functions. It solves the problem of expensive Monte Carlo integration for spherical integrals by converting them into efficient, differentiable algebraic operations (dot products and matrix-vector multiplications) on coefficient vectors. It is chosen when low-frequency directional signals need to be evaluated repeatedly in inverse problems or real-time rendering, trading high-frequency detail for computational speed and differentiability.
+Targets the computational efficiency of the *product* of functions (e.g., visibility $\times$ lighting) within inverse problems. It sets itself apart by precomputing transfer matrices and using linear algebra to evaluate integrals, avoiding Monte Carlo methods. It specifically handles geometry-dependent terms like hemisphere response via rotation matrices to maintain differentiability.
 
