 ### H-Basis Encoding
-H-Basis is a basis used to encode a hemispherical signal, requiring fewer coefficients to achieve the same quality as 3rd order spherical harmonics [S3, unofficial]. A coordinate system local to the surface is required to orient the hemisphere, commonly utilizing a tangent frame resulting from uv-parameterization [S3, unofficial]. If encoded in textures, the texture needs sufficient resolution to adapt to changes in the underlying tangent space [S3, unofficial]. If two triangles cover the same texel and have significantly different tangents, it leads to a lack of precision in the reconstructed signal [S3, unofficial].
+**Definition:** A basis used to encode a hemispherical signal, requiring fewer coefficients to achieve the same quality as 3rd order spherical harmonics [S3, unofficial].
+**Step:** A coordinate system local to the surface is required to orient the hemisphere, commonly utilizing a tangent frame resulting from uv-parameterization [S3, unofficial].
+**Pitfall:** If encoded in textures, the texture needs sufficient resolution to adapt to changes in the underlying tangent space. If two triangles cover the same texel and have significantly different tangents, it leads to a lack of precision in the reconstructed signal [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Spherical Harmonics are a complete basis of functions mapping the unit sphere $S^2$ to the complex numbers $\mathbb{C}$ [S1, unofficial]. They are a set of special functions defined on a sphere, used to efficiently describe arbitrary functions on the sphere surface [S2, unofficial]. In computer graphics, they are widely applied to represent lighting, reflection, and compress environment illumination [S2, unofficial]. Specific applications include representing ambient occlusion for static environment light, compressing global illumination low-frequency components, and representing Bidirectional Reflectance Distribution Functions (BRDF) [S2, unofficial]. The technique is used in real-time rendering, such as baked lightmaps in *Call of Duty: Infinite Warfare* [S3, unofficial].
+Spherical Harmonics are a set of special functions defined on a sphere, used to efficiently describe arbitrary functions on the sphere surface [S2, unofficial]. In computer graphics, they are widely applied to represent lighting, reflection, and compress environment illumination [S2, unofficial]. They are particularly effective for capturing low-frequency lighting components, such as ambient occlusion and global illumination, allowing for efficient rendering of complex lighting conditions [S2, unofficial]. The use of SH in rendering equations for inverse problems and neural radiance fields (NeRFs) has been popularized in recent years, with implementations like PlenOctrees demonstrating their utility in real-time rendering [S1, unofficial].
 
